      "section": "conclusion",
      "text": "Conclusion This study demonstrates the effectiveness of ultrasonication for degrading PFAS in various water matrices, including MQ, SW, CW, and GW. Degradation and defluorination varied significantly with water type and ionic background. Notably, defluorination rates were consis - tently lower than removal rates, indicating that fluorine often remains trapped in stable intermediates or that degradation was not yet complete. Ultrasonication proved especially effective in low-salinity environ - ments like CW, where the highest defluorination was observed. In contrast, SW showed the lowest efficiency, highlighting the inhibitory effect of high salinity on cavitation and radical generation. These results underscore the need for context-specific strategies: ultrasonication may serve as a standalone solution in freshwater systems but may require integration with other technologies in saline waters. Importantly, this study shows that ultrasonication performs competitively with several AOPs. It has comparable or even superior degradation rate constants, especially for PFOS in SW, along with operational simplicity, suggesting it is a viable and practical alternative for treating PFAS-contaminated environmental waters. Finally, the challenge of achieving complete mass balance remains. Improvements in analytical methods, such as expanding the TOP assay to detect ultrashort-chain PFAS like TFA and PFPrA, are needed to better account for all degradation products. Future research should also focus on identifying unknown precursors and transformation intermediates to"
